WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.090
Here’s a puzzle.

2
00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.680
Every time you sign in, a

3
00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.400
website checks your password.

4
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.560
But a well-built site never

5
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.940
keeps your password at all.

6
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.670
So how does it know you

7
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.930
typed the right one?

8
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.510
Today: fingerprints of data — what a

9
00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.070
hash is, and how defenders use it.

10
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.090
A hash function turns any data — a

11
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:28.910
word, a photo, a whole film — into a

12
00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:31.010
short fingerprint of fixed length.

13
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.150
One of the most used, SHA-256, always

14
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.430
makes two hundred and fifty-six bits,

15
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:39.410
written as sixty-four characters.

16
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.840
A good hash keeps three rules.

17
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.640
The same input always gives

18
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:46.660
the same fingerprint.

19
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.780
Change a single letter, and about

20
00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.920
half of its bits flip: the

21
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.940
fingerprint looks completely new.

22
00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.490
And it only works one way: from the

23
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.970
fingerprint, nobody can work back to the

24
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:00.270
input.

25
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.110
It’s like a blender.

26
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.640
The same mangoes always

27
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.920
make the same juice.

28
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.240
But nobody can turn the

29
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.220
juice back into mangoes.

30
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.770
Here’s how the website solves our puzzle.

31
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.080
When you sign up, it stores only the

32
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.360
fingerprint of your password.

33
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.760
When you sign in, it hashes what you type

34
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.680
and compares the two fingerprints.

35
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.250
If they match, you’re in — and a

36
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.030
thief who steals the list gets

37
00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.590
fingerprints, not passwords.

38
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:33.540
Quick check!

39
00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.780
I change just one letter in a long

40
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.720
message, then hash it again.

41
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.940
What happens to the fingerprint?

42
00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.130
About half of its bits change.

43
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.960
Look: hello with a small h, and

44
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.080
Hello with a capital, give

45
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.400
fingerprints that share almost nothing.

46
00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.450
That’s why a hash is such a good

47
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.350
alarm — one changed letter, and the

48
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.870
fingerprints no longer match.

49
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.420
Defenders use fingerprints every day.

50
00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.020
Official downloads publish their

51
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.760
fingerprint, so you can check the

52
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.320
file wasn’t changed on the way.

53
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.500
Backup tools compare fingerprints to

54
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.240
make sure a copy is perfect.

55
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.350
And investigators record the fingerprint

56
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.910
of evidence, to show it was never touched.

57
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:22.480
But a stolen list of

58
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.480
fingerprints still matters.

59
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.490
Attackers hash millions of common

60
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.090
passwords and look for matches, so

61
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.650
“password123” falls at once.

62
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.680
Good sites add a random salt and use a

63
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.880
slow hash, so every guess costs time.

64
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.980
Your long, unique

65
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.800
passphrase does the rest.

66
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:45.540
Your turn.

67
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.510
A website emails you your old

68
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.810
password, “in case you forgot it”.

69
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.500
Good sign or bad sign?

70
00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.360
A bad sign: if a site can send your

71
00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.220
password back, it’s storing the

72
00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.020
password itself, not a fingerprint.

73
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.170
Remember: a hash is a one-way

74
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:07.350
fingerprint, the same every time, and

75
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.590
one tiny change makes a new one.

76
00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.890
Defenders use it to store passwords and

77
00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.350
to prove that nothing changed.

78
00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.160
Try it tomorrow: in the lab, hash

79
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.900
your name, change one letter, and

80
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.140
compare the two fingerprints.

81
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.400
Then find the fingerprint printed next to

82
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.020
a download on an official website, and

83
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.760
explain to a friend what it’s for.
